{"article_ids": ["192c2ef3c0473227"], "canonical_title": "TraceSpec: Understanding and Improving Speculative Decoding for LLM Agents", "category": "research", "confidence": 0.9, "description": "The paper introduces TraceSpec, a framework that ties evaluation of LLM agent execution trajectories to adaptive training of draft models used in speculative decoding. It merges dynamic execution with fixed‑prefix replay and releases a new dataset called TraceSpec‑Traj containing many agent trajectories.", "entities": [], "first_seen": "2026-11-02T05:00:00Z", "id": "evt_192c2ef3c0473227", "importance_score": 6.04, "last_updated": "2026-11-02T05:00:00Z", "source_ids": ["arxiv-cs-cl"]}
{"article_ids": ["1549edabf6f2bea6"], "canonical_title": "On the Origin and Evolution of Training-Inference Mismatch in LLM Reinforcement Learning", "category": "research", "confidence": 0.68, "description": "Researchers observed that on‑policy reinforcement learning for large language models suffers a mismatch because inference engines sample responses separately from the trainer that computes gradients. The mismatch originates from engine numerics and stale weights in asynchronous sampling. The paper describes this issue and outlines a correction method.", "entities": [], "first_seen": "2026-11-02T05:00:00Z", "id": "evt_1549edabf6f2bea6", "importance_score": 5.84, "last_updated": "2026-11-02T05:00:00Z", "source_ids": ["arxiv-cs-lg"]}
{"article_ids": ["36ffed99634dd2dd"], "canonical_title": "Capability-Grounded Agentic Planning", "category": "research", "confidence": 0.95, "description": "The paper presents Capability-Grounded Agentic Planning (CGAP), a method where large language model (LLM) agents reason about the capabilities required by a natural‑language request and match them to available tools. CGAP compiles the tool catalog into a deterministic PDDL (Planning Domain Definition Language) model, replacing the usual trial‑and‑error selection of tool calls with a structured, executable plan.", "entities": ["term:cgap"], "first_seen": "2026-11-02T05:00:00Z", "id": "evt_36ffed99634dd2dd", "importance_score": 5.84, "last_updated": "2026-11-02T05:00:00Z", "source_ids": ["arxiv-cs-ai"]}
{"article_ids": ["82ec853666f8de30"], "canonical_title": "KEEPER: Learning Control-Sufficient Textual Memory for Long-Horizon LLM Agents", "category": "research", "confidence": 0.68, "description": "The paper titled \"KEEPER: Learning Control-Sufficient Textual Memory for Long-Horizon LLM Agents\" defines a problem called self-consistency bias, where incorrect claims persist in a recursive textual memory. It proposes the KEEPER framework, which evaluates four auxiliary training objectives positioned along information-content and update-sensitivity dimensions within a shared recursive interface, to study how different supervision targets affect memory reliability.", "entities": ["term:keeper"], "first_seen": "2026-11-02T05:00:00Z", "id": "evt_82ec853666f8de30", "importance_score": 5.79, "last_updated": "2026-11-02T05:00:00Z", "source_ids": ["arxiv-cs-cl"]}
{"article_ids": ["1273c4ddef8dc14a"], "canonical_title": "CIRCE Divines LLM Task Complexity from Constraint Structure", "category": "research", "confidence": 0.8, "description": "The arXiv paper titled \"CIRCE Divines LLM Task Complexity from Constraint Structure\" introduces a method that estimates whether a problem is too complex for a large language model before the model attempts it. Unlike prior estimators that rely on problem text, LLM‑annotated difficulty labels, or post‑attempt signals, CIRCE explicitly leverages the constraint structure of the task to predict difficulty.", "entities": ["term:circe"], "first_seen": "2026-11-02T05:00:00Z", "id": "evt_1273c4ddef8dc14a", "importance_score": 5.64, "last_updated": "2026-11-02T05:00:00Z", "source_ids": ["arxiv-cs-ai"]}
